{"analyses":[{"paper_id":"doi-10-1177-002383099603900306","title":"Inferring Acceptance and Rejection in Dialogue by Default Rules of Inference","research_question":"How can conversational participants infer acceptance or rejection of assertions and proposals when responses are indirect, logically consistent with what they reject, or only partially accept the preceding propositional content?","methodology":"The paper combines corpus analysis, discourse/pragmatic analysis, prosodic analysis, and formal modeling. Walker examines a five-hour corpus of 55 live financial-advice dialogues, supplemented with examples from the literature, identifying 43 acceptance examples and 31 rejection examples; she also defines informationally redundant utterances (IRUs) and codes 184 IRUs for factors including logical relation, antecedent distance, speaker relation, phrase-final tone, and intonational contour. Distributional and prosodic patterns are analyzed, including a reported chi-square test, and naturally occurring examples are used to develop a taxonomy of rejection. The empirical observations are then incorporated into a default-inference model of common ground that distinguishes hypotheses, defaults, and more strongly supported propositions.","key_findings":[{"finding":"Logical consistency is necessary but not sufficient for interpreting a response as acceptance, while logical inconsistency is sufficient but not necessary for interpreting a response as rejection.","evidence_type":"supported","section":"1 Introduction; 7 Conclusion"},{"finding":"A reply can accept an entailed subproposition of a preceding assertion while rejecting the stronger assertion, so acceptance and rejection scope can differ within the same antecedent proposition.","evidence_type":"supported","section":"1 Introduction; 5 Adding to the Common Ground; 6 Discussion of Open Issues and Future Work"},{"finding":"The corpus analysis motivates three rejection classes beyond straightforward denial, contradiction, and refusal: implicature rejections, epistemic rejections, and deliberation rejections.","evidence_type":"supported","section":"3 Inferring Rejection in Dialogue; 7 Conclusion"},{"finding":"A less informative reply can reject only the omitted or replaced portion of a stronger antecedent through scalar implicature rather than rejecting every proposition entailed by the antecedent.","evidence_type":"supported","section":"3.1.1 Rejection by Implicature; 6 Discussion of Open Issues and Future Work"},{"finding":"Information structure and prosody help distinguish a partial repetition functioning as acceptance from a superficially similar logically consistent utterance functioning as rejection; acceptances tend to re-realize focal material as old information, whereas implicature rejections substitute a scalarly related focal item.","evidence_type":"supported","section":"2.2 Distributional Analysis of Acceptances; 4 The Role of Information Structure in distinguishing Acceptance and Implicature Rejection; 7 Conclusion"},{"finding":"IRUs may have more than one discourse antecedent; the paper notes examples where a response depends on two preceding utterances, although its corpus coding operationally treats the most recent utterance as the IRU antecedent in such cases.","evidence_type":"supported","section":"2.2 Distributional Analysis of Acceptances"},{"finding":"The proposed common-ground model represents partial acceptance by adding the proposition explicitly realized by the response while allowing a default rejection inference to defeat acceptance of the stronger antecedent.","evidence_type":"unsupported","section":"5 Adding to the Common Ground; 6 Discussion of Open Issues and Future Work"},{"finding":"Fall-Rise intonation can assist in identifying rejection, but it is neither necessary nor sufficient for rejection by itself.","evidence_type":"supported","section":"4.2 Fall-Rise as an Indicator of Rejection"}],"limitations":["The empirical corpus is small and domain-specific: 55 financial-advice dialogues comprising about five hours of speech.","Only 31 rejection instances were identified in the 55 dialogues, including 19 rejections of assertions and 12 rejections of proposals.","Acceptance and rejection classifications were based on the author's interpretations; no independent annotation or inter-annotator agreement is reported.","The author explicitly states that the proposed classification should be tested on a larger corpus with speech available.","Explicit forms such as direct yes/agreement and no/refusal are deliberately excluded from much of the linguistic-cue analysis.","The adequacy of the formal representation of partial acceptance remains an open issue; the paper discusses alternative modifications but does not resolve them.","Although multiple antecedents are acknowledged, the operational corpus treatment chooses the most recent utterance as the antecedent, so genuinely multi-antecedent response-link representation is not developed.","No systematic treatment of multi-question antecedents or unanswered subquestions is provided."],"reproducibility":{"code_available":false,"dataset_available":false,"metrics_reported":true},"confidence_scores":{"logical_consistency_not_sufficient":0.98,"partial_acceptance_with_rejection":0.96,"three_rejection_classes":0.96,"scalar_scope_of_rejection":0.92,"information_structure_and_prosody":0.93,"multiple_IRU_antecedents":0.9,"common_ground_partial_acceptance_model":0.77,"fall_rise_is_only_a_cue":0.95},"raw_claims":["Responses that are logically consistent with an antecedent can nevertheless function as rejections.","A response may accept a weaker proposition entailed by an antecedent while rejecting the antecedent's stronger content.","The financial-advice corpus contains substantially fewer rejection examples than acceptance-related material.","Rejections of assertions and rejections of proposals require partly different kinds of inference.","Scalar implicature can make an informationally weaker response reject a stronger preceding assertion.","Epistemic rejection can arise from default reasoning about another speaker's beliefs rather than direct contradiction of uttered content.","Deliberation rejection can arise from reasoning about intentions, alternatives, consequences, and the desirability of proposed actions.","Adjacent informationally redundant utterances by the other speaker occupy a position associated with expression of attitude toward the prior contribution.","Some informationally redundant responses have multiple preceding utterances contributing antecedent content.","Prosodic realization provides evidence for distinguishing acceptance functions from other uses of informationally redundant utterances.","Fall-Rise intonation is a useful but non-determinative cue to rejection.","The proposed common-ground representation keeps acceptance defeasible long enough for conflicting default rejection inferences to override it."],"source_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/cmp-lg/9609002","access":"full_text"},{"paper_id":"doi-10-1515-ling-2018-0008","title":"Resolving abstract anaphors in Spanish discourse: Underspecification and mereological structures","research_question":"How can hearers resolve an abstract anaphor when several individual propositions, events, or a merged multi-proposition discourse segment are all plausible antecedents?","methodology":"The paper is primarily a theoretical and descriptive discourse-semantic study rather than an experimental evaluation. Zulaica analyzes Spanish discourse examples, including naturally occurring material from sources such as the Corpus de Referencia del Español Actual, by segmenting discourse into propositions and representing candidate antecedents as atomic and merged abstract referents. The analysis combines mereological representations inspired by the Justified Sloppiness Hypothesis with Segmented Discourse Representation Theory to model rhetorical relations and discourse topics. From these analyses the author proposes a hypothetical three-step cognitive resolution process, without testing it through a new psycholinguistic experiment or computational benchmark.","key_findings":[{"finding":"Abstract anaphors can be underspecified among multiple higher-order antecedents, including individual propositions, events, and larger multi-sentence discourse segments.","evidence_type":"supported","section":"1 Introduction; 2 Discourse segments, abstract referents, and collective reference"},{"finding":"The candidate set for an abstract anaphor can be represented mereologically so that atomic propositions and one or more merged complex abstract referents remain simultaneously available as possible interpretations.","evidence_type":"supported","section":"1 Introduction; 3 Mereological structures and the Justified Sloppiness Hypothesis"},{"finding":"The paper proposes a three-step resolution process: construct a mereological set of possible antecedents, infer rhetorical relations among its propositions, and select the preferred antecedent for the anaphor.","evidence_type":"unsupported","section":"1 Introduction; 5 Conclusions"},{"finding":"When several thematically connected propositions can be integrated into a coherent rhetorical unit, their merged interpretation is proposed to be preferred over its individual component propositions.","evidence_type":"unsupported","section":"1 Introduction; 4 Propositional integration and rhetorical structure; 5 Conclusions"},{"finding":"Rhetorical structure can determine proposition-level antecedent scope: a sequence connected by Continuation can form the merged referent of an anaphor while a nearby proposition connected by a different relation can remain outside that referent.","evidence_type":"supported","section":"4 Propositional integration and rhetorical structure"},{"finding":"Narration and Continuation are proposed as especially conducive to forming merged discourse units, often functioning within a broader Elaboration relation to a discourse topic.","evidence_type":"unsupported","section":"4 Propositional integration and rhetorical structure; 5 Conclusions"},{"finding":"If no suitable rhetorical relation supports a merged interpretation, the proposed mechanism falls back to an atomic proposition in the candidate mereological structure.","evidence_type":"unsupported","section":"1 Introduction; 4 Propositional integration and rhetorical structure"},{"finding":"Discourse-topic construction is treated as incremental: new propositions can be integrated into an evolving complex segment rather than requiring an anaphor to link to only one immediately preceding proposition.","evidence_type":"supported","section":"4 Propositional integration and rhetorical structure"}],"limitations":["The central three-step resolution mechanism is explicitly presented as a hypothesis rather than as an experimentally validated cognitive model.","The study relies mainly on detailed analysis of selected discourse examples rather than a systematically sampled and quantitatively evaluated corpus of abstract-anaphor cases.","No new psycholinguistic experiment tests whether hearers actually prefer the predicted merged antecedents or follow the proposed processing sequence.","No computational resolver is implemented, so there are no accuracy, precision, recall, or baseline-comparison results.","The paper explicitly calls for future empirical psychological research on underspecification involving multi-sentence antecedents.","The role of discourse connectives and discourse markers in promoting mereological interpretations remains an open research question.","Differences among noun-phrase, pronominal, and demonstrative abstract anaphors in encouraging merged interpretations are left for future investigation.","The semantic ontology is intentionally simplified primarily to propositions and events rather than distinguishing finer categories of abstract objects.","The analysis concerns anaphoric reference rather than response acts, so it does not directly test approval, rejection, unanswered questions, or partial answers in workplace communication."],"reproducibility":{"code_available":false,"dataset_available":false,"metrics_reported":false},"confidence_scores":{"multiple_higher_order_antecedents":0.98,"mereological_candidate_structure":0.96,"three_step_resolution_process":0.69,"merged_interpretation_preference":0.67,"rhetorical_scope_selection":0.91,"narration_continuation_facilitate_merging":0.7,"fallback_to_atomic_referent":0.75,"incremental_multi_proposition_topic":0.9},"raw_claims":["An abstract anaphor may have several propositions or events as plausible antecedents.","A sequence of propositions can itself constitute a complex abstract antecedent.","Atomic propositions and their merged interpretation can coexist in one candidate antecedent structure.","The proposed resolution process first keeps multiple interpretations available instead of forcing an immediate single-antecedent decision.","Rhetorical relations are used to decide whether propositions form a coherent merged discourse unit.","A merged interpretation is proposed to be especially salient when it represents a coherent general purpose of the speaker.","Continuation can join multiple propositions into a complex segment that an abstract anaphor subsequently denotes.","A proposition located near an anaphor need not belong to its antecedent if rhetorical structure places that proposition outside the relevant complex segment.","Discourse topics can be incrementally updated as further thematically related propositions are processed.","When no coherent merged referent can be constructed, an individual proposition remains available as the anaphor's antecedent.","The paper treats the three-step mechanism as a hypothesis and explicitly identifies empirical validation of multi-sentence antecedent resolution as future work."],"source_url":"https://scholarworks.indianapolis.iu.edu/bitstream/1805/18711/1/Zulaica_2018_resolving.pdf","access":"full_text"}],"skipped":[]}
===END shard-3.result.json===
